FujiNet Go MSX (desktop) -- what is done and what is next
===========================================================

The port follows fujinet-go-adam-desktop/PORTING.md, whose milestone order
(section 12) this file adapts. The derivation is

    msx-desktop = (adam-desktop - adam-android) + msx-android

with the CoCo port as the closer model for the "FujiNet listens, emulator
connects out" transport direction and the variable-frame-geometry pattern,
and ADAM as the closer model for the debugger engine (the MSX1 VDP is
literally a TMS9918A, so ADAM's vdpview.c is the widened base rather than a
reference).

MSX diverges from every sibling in one structural way the other three don't:
openMSX is not staged and compiled by this project's own CMake the way
adamcore/AppleWin/XRoar are. It is 570 C++23 translation units with a real
3rd-party dependency chain (Tcl, SDL2, SDL2_ttf, freetype, libpng, zlib,
ogg/vorbis/theora, GLEW), and openMSX's own GNUmakefile + build/3rdparty
chain already solves that per-platform -- the same chain the Android sibling
already drives successfully. cmake/StageOpenMSX.cmake stages + patches the
source, then invokes that chain rather than reinventing it. See
COMPLIANCE.md for the patch list.

Two more MSX-specific decisions, both settled before any code was written:
  * openMSX owns its own SDL2 Mixer audio and its own msxjoystick1/2 plugs.
    This app links no SDL3 at all -- core/src/audio_sdl.c and
    core/src/gamepad_sdl.c from the sibling repos are NOT ported.
  * Frame capture cannot use the Android app's -Wl,--wrap,SDL_GL_SwapWindow
    + glReadPixels trick (GNU ld/lld only, absent on macOS). The desktop tap
    is a hook in PostProcessor::rotateFrames() (src/video/PostProcessor.cc),
    openMSX's own per-frame CPU-side consumer of the finished FrameSource,
    reusing the getScaledFrame() helper already in that file. No GL readback
    at all.

Ports, fixed for this target (never 1985/1986/8055, which the Android
sibling and a standalone fujinet-pc-msx already use):
  BoIP (FujiBus over TCP, FujiNet listens, openMSX connects out): 65505
  Web UI:                                                         64003


Done (this pass) -- M0, M1
--------------------------

M1
  * cmake/Dependencies.cmake -- msx_provide_dependency(), ported from
    CoCo's coco_provide_dependency() and simplified: openMSX and
    fujinet-firmware are both live git forks with real history (unlike
    CoCo's XRoar tarball), so both resolve via the SRC-override / clone-at-
    pin path, with no .gitmodules registration (matches what CoCo's
    third_party/ actually does at runtime, despite its own comment saying
    "submodule" -- there is no .gitmodules there either). openMSX pinned at
    b11b550120fe9d342f9bd64128de7bd06e1a7758 (feat/fujinet), fujinet-firmware
    at b9a27cfa145134f5a7088535ac2721eb3cdf41be.
  * cmake/StageOpenMSX.cmake -- stages src/, build/, share/ and
    Contrib/cbios into core/openmsx-generated/, then runs
    tools/openmsx/patches/patch-staged-tree.py. Re-stages on source or
    patch-script change, or -DOPENMSX_RESTAGE=ON.
  * tools/openmsx/patches/patch-staged-tree.py -- two patches, both
    anchor-verified and confirmed idempotent by running twice against the
    real checkout:
      - src/serial/FujiNet.cc: FUJINET_DEFAULT_PORT 1985 -> 65505.
      - src/video/PostProcessor.cc: a msxhost_notify_frame(paintFrame) call
        inserted in rotateFrames(), right beside openMSX's own AVI-recorder
        tap (proof this is the right per-frame CPU-side hook point) --
        forward-declares FrameSource and the extern "C" hook once, near the
        top of the file.
  * cmake/OpenMSXRuntime.cmake + tools/openmsx/build-openmsx-desktop.sh --
    drives openMSX's own GNUmakefile over the staged+patched tree via bash
    (never a bare .sh), tolerates the expected final-executable-link
    failure (msxhost_notify_frame is undefined until M2 supplies it, same
    as the Android sibling tolerates its own link failure for a different
    reason), archives every object except main.o into libopenmsx.a, and
    writes include-dirs.txt from a `find src -type d` that mirrors
    openMSX's own SOURCE_DIRS logic (build/main.mk:260) so a future
    openMSX version adding a subdirectory doesn't silently break the
    include path. Linux links system Tcl/SDL2/freetype/etc dynamically
    (LINK_MODE=SYS_DYN, the default); darwin/mingw-w64 will additionally
    run `make 3rdparty` first for static linking (untested so far -- no
    Mac/Windows box; M7/M9 concern).
  * cmake/toolchains/mingw-w64.cmake -- ported from CoCo, scoped to
    cross-compiling the *session/frontend* C/C++ code, not openMSX itself
    (which needs a real MSYS2/UCRT64 shell for its own 3rdparty chain --
    windows.yml in CI builds natively, matching the family convention).
  * cmake/FujiNetRuntime.cmake -- ported verbatim from CoCo (renamed),
    unexercised until M3 (WITH_FUJINET=OFF so far).
  * CMakeLists.txt, core/CMakeLists.txt -- skeleton; core/tests/
    openmsx_link_test.cpp links openmsx-lib and calls the real
    openmsx::Version::full() (deliberately not just an empty translation
    unit against the headers, so a link-only build tells the real story).

  VERIFIED end to end, twice:
    1. Against the local OPENMSX_SRC=~/Workspace/openMSX checkout:
       staged, patched (both patches applied and confirmed idempotent),
       571 objects compiled, archived into libopenmsx.a, our own
       openmsx_link_test linked and ran ("openMSX 21.0-unknown" -- the
       "unknown" is expected, the staged tree has no .git metadata, same
       known condition PORTING.md documents for FujiNet's
       version_common.py -- not a defect here).
    2. The actual clean-clone gate: a fresh `git clone --local` of this
       repository (no OPENMSX_SRC override), submodule-init step failing
       gracefully as expected (no .gitmodules) and falling through to the
       clone-at-pin path, which checked out EXACTLY
       b11b550120fe9d342f9bd64128de7bd06e1a7758 (confirmed with git
       rev-parse), then staged/patched/compiled/archived/linked
       identically to run 1. (The clone was pointed at the local openMSX
       checkout's path rather than GitHub, purely to avoid a slow network
       fetch of the real ~580MB history during verification -- the code
       path exercised, and the pin match confirmed, are the real thing;
       only the URL was swapped for speed, and only in a throwaway
       scratch clone, never committed.)


Done (this pass) -- M0
-----------------------

  * git init, LICENSE (GPL-3.0, verbatim from fujinet-go-coco-desktop),
    .gitignore (build*/ with !build-aux/, core/openmsx-generated/,
    third_party/{openmsx,fujinet-firmware}/, tools/*/work/,
    .flatpak-builder/).
  * README.md -- family framing, planned-features list, MSX system ROM
    story (C-BIOS bundled unconditionally -- this is the one target in the
    family that gets to have that), status table (all four frontends "not
    started"/"in progress").
  * COMPLIANCE.md -- full per-component table, and in particular:
      - The openMSX licence determination (GPL-2.0-or-later, matching the
        Android sibling's call on the same ambiguous README wording) is
        written as a determination, not a quotation.
      - The three openMSX staged-source patches (BoIP port, the
        rotateFrames() frame hook, and the explicit *omission* of the
        Android sibling's touch-keyboard EventDelay patch -- a physical
        keyboard needs no release-delay hack).
      - C-BIOS bundled / turboR-and-real-machine ROMs never bundled, same
        enforcement shape as CoCo's no_embedded_roms.py (adapted: asserts
        absence rather than gating an ON/OFF option, since MSX has nothing
        to gate -- C-BIOS is always in).
  * No PORTING.md in this repo -- confirmed neither coco-desktop nor
    apple2-desktop carry their own copy either; only adam-desktop (the
    model repo) has one, and this file (TODO) is where the other three
    repos actually record per-repo decisions and status. README points at
    adam-desktop's PORTING.md for the shared specification.
  * GitHub repo NOT created/pushed yet -- local commits only until the
    maintainer says otherwise.


Done (this pass) -- M2
----------------------

  * core/openmsx/msx_host.{cc,hh} -- ported from the Android sibling's
    msx_host.cpp, Android-only halves dropped (no audio ring, no
    -Wl,--wrap trick, no JNI -- see the M2 commit message and
    COMPLIANCE.md). Reactor boot (-machine <id> -ext FujiNet, powerOn,
    run on its own thread) with a real success/failure handshake back to
    the caller (msxhost_core_start() blocks until known, unlike the
    Android host's fire-and-forget spawn). SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen
    headless GL context on Linux; macOS/Windows headless GL are still
    untested (no Mac/Windows box -- M7/M9). msxhost_notify_frame() packs
    the FrameSource into XRGB8888 via getLinePtr640_480() only -- see
    below, this ended up simpler than originally planned.
  * core/src/session.c + core/include/msxsession.h -- thin lifecycle
    wrapper (no phase-locked pacer: openMSX paces itself, `throttle on` +
    `fullspeedwhenloading off` so FujiNet's M3 "loading" hold won't race
    emulated time), the latest-frame double-buffer, key/joystick
    passthrough. msxsession_notify_vsync() is advisory only
    (MSX_PACE_LOG=1 diagnostic). No audio API at all -- openMSX owns its
    own SDL2 Mixer straight to the real device; this app links no SDL3
    (confirmed: audio_sdl.c/gamepad_sdl.c from the sibling repos are NOT
    ported, exactly as planned).
  * core/src/input_map.c -- msx_key_from_event(), X11 keysym ->
    SDL_Keycode + mod bits. No MSX keyboard-matrix table needed: openMSX's
    own Keyboard::processKeyEvent does that translation already.
  * frontends/gnome/{main,window,display}.c -- window + display + keyboard
    capture, Reset action, F11 fullscreen. M2 scope only: no menu beyond
    Reset/About (media import, machine switching, FujiNet config,
    debugger, preferences all land in M3-M6).
  * cmake/OpenMSXRuntime.cmake -- Linux dynamic-link deps (SDL2,
    SDL2_ttf, libpng, ogg/theora/vorbis, GLEW, zlib, GL, ALSA, freetype,
    Tcl) added, matching `ldd` on a native openMSX build exactly.

  CORRECTED from the original M2 plan (both discovered by testing, not
  by re-reading the plan):
    1. msxhost_notify_frame() only needs getLinePtr640_480() -- not a
       getLinePtr320_240()-vs-640_480() choice keyed off the source
       resolution. That API scales *any* source frame to a fixed
       640x480 unconditionally (it exists for exactly this purpose --
       video recording), so msxsession's frame geometry is FIXED, not
       variable like the sibling targets. This simplified
       msxsession_copy_frame() (no w_out/h_out) and display.c (no
       per-frame texture resize) versus what was planned.
    2. The plan predicted "openMSX's own executable link now succeeds"
       once msxhost_notify_frame is defined. This was wrong and stays
       wrong forever, not just for M2: that symbol is defined in
       msx_host.cc, which is part of libmsxsession, and is NEVER linked
       into openMSX's own derived/<platform>/bin/openmsx executable (we
       only ever extract *objects* from that build, never link the
       executable itself). build-openmsx-desktop.sh's tolerated-failure
       branch is a permanent, expected feature of this port, not an
       M1-only condition -- every future milestone's build log will keep
       showing it, and that is correct.

  A REAL BUG caught by the clean-clone gate test (see the fix commit
  after M2's): cmake/OpenMSXRuntime.cmake computed msxsession's
  per-subdirectory openMSX include path from a file
  (tools/openmsx/work/out/include-dirs.txt) that only exists after
  build-openmsx-desktop.sh has already run once -- but a target's
  INTERFACE_INCLUDE_DIRECTORIES is baked in at CMake *generate* time,
  which happens once per configure and is never revisited just because a
  later build step writes a new file. On this development machine the
  bug was invisible for both the M1 and initial M2 verification passes,
  because build/ already had a populated include-dirs.txt left over from
  an earlier successful build in the same tree -- it only showed up when
  a genuine `git clone --local` into an *empty* directory was tried,
  which is exactly why that gate exists and is worth re-running for real
  after every milestone, not just trusted from memory. Fixed by
  computing the subdirectory list from the staged source tree directly
  (populated earlier in the same configure pass by StageOpenMSX.cmake)
  instead of a build-time artifact.

  VERIFIED end to end:
    - boot_smoke (new ctest): C-BIOS MSX2 boots headless, 30 real
      non-blank 640x480 frames in 1.45s.
    - The GNOME frontend launched under a real X11 display (DISPLAY=:1),
      ran 36s, no crash, no MSX-specific error (only this system's
      pre-existing GTK/libadwaita theme-CSS warnings, unrelated).
    - Both ctest cases pass from a fresh `rm -rf build` rebuild AND from
      a genuine `git clone --local` into an empty directory with no
      OPENMSX_SRC override (the include-path bug above was caught and
      fixed via exactly this second check).


Next -- M3
----------

FujiNet in-process: tools/fujinet/build-fujinet-desktop.sh ported from
coco (~95% target-independent per PORTING.md), PC_TARGET=RS232, BoIP
65505, web UI 64003. THE LONG POLE per the original milestone plan.
Gate: press 'c' from C-BIOS, FujiNet CONFIG screen shows a populated
host list; ss -tnp shows one ESTABLISHED pair on 65505.


Then, in order (M4 through M9)
-------------------------------

  M4  Settings store, media import (.dsk/.rom/.cas -> SD folder, carts ->
      cart dir), Import System ROMs (turboR/real-machine), machine profile
      switching (MSX/MSX2/MSX2+/turboR) with clean restart, joystick
      binding driven through openMSX Tcl (plug joyporta msxjoystick1,
      joystick1_config) via the command queue.

  M5  KDE frontend (Qt6 Widgets). Doing this early is what keeps the core
      honest about staying toolkit-agnostic.

  M6  Debugger engine: z80dasm.{c,h} ported verbatim from adam-desktop
      (same CPU, already tested). Pause/step/continue call
      MSXCPUInterface::doBreak/doStep/doContinue directly; breakpoints via
      Tcl set_bp/remove_bp/list_bp; snapshot reads via
      debug read_block <debuggable> <offset> <size> against VRAM,
      physical VRAM, VDP regs (0x40 -- R0-R46, so the command engine
      registers need no separate source), VDP status regs, VDP palette
      (RBG order, not RGB) and VDP VRAM pointer. msxvdpview.c/msxvdp.h
      widen adam-desktop's vdpview.c/adamvdp_* to V9938/V9958: full mode
      table (TEXT1/2, MULTICOLOR, GRAPHIC1-7 + YJK/YAE), bitmap-mode
      screen rendering, sprite mode 2, palette RAM, 17-bit VRAM, and a new
      command-engine pane (SX/SY/DX/DY/NX/NY/CLR/ARG/CMR + S#2 CE/TR).
      symbols_builtin.c gets an MSX BIOS/BDOS table (CHGET, CHPUT, WRTVDP,
      LDIRVM, ...) instead of ADAM's EOS/OS7. GNOME debugger window ported
      from adam-desktop with the VDP tab's command-engine addition.

  M7  CI: linux.yml first (nm -D libfujinet.so), then macos.yml (otool -L
      every Mach-O, fail on /opt/homebrew or /usr/local) and windows.yml
      (objdump -p import table, NOT ldd), then flatpak.yml (tcl, sdl2,
      sdl2_ttf, freetype, libpng, glew added as declared modules -- no
      network during the sandboxed build, so anything undeclared is
      simply absent).

  M8  Icons (tools/icons/make-icons.py against fujinet-go-msx's launcher
      art), desktop entries, AppStream metainfo, CPack DEB/RPM/TGZ,
      build-aux/windows/installer.nsi.

  M9  macOS (AppKit) + Windows (Win32) frontends and their debugger
      windows, release.yml, v0.1.0.


Known issues / things to remember
----------------------------------

* openMSX's README states the GPL with no explicit "or later" clause
  (GPL.txt shipped is v2 text). Treated as GPL-2.0-or-later per the
  Android sibling's precedent -- see COMPLIANCE.md's determination
  section, and flag it the same way if this ever needs re-litigating.
* The FujiNet cartridge device (src/serial/FujiNet.cc) already retries its
  outbound connect once per second on failure, so unlike CoCo's XRoar
  becker.c the desktop port does NOT need a lazy-connect source patch here
  -- upstream's own retry loop is sufficient. Confirm this holds once M3
  is under real load; if it doesn't, the CoCo becker.c patch is the model
  to follow.
* A *failed* renderer init must abort the boot before powerOn() --
  openMSX's VDP::reset() dereferences a null renderer on a half-up video
  system. Carry the same guard the Android sibling already has.
* SDLGL-PP is the only real renderer RendererFactory maps (besides DUMMY),
  so a GL context of some kind is unavoidable even though the pixels are
  captured on the CPU side via rotateFrames() rather than read back with
  glReadPixels. Don't try to drop GL entirely without first checking how
  much of SDLVideoSystem (ImGui/OSD/snow layers, all built over an
  OutputSurface) that would actually require patching -- PostProcessor is
  final and it's more surgery than this port should promise. Revisit only
  as a later simplification, not as part of the initial port.
